{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"docs-capital-compliance-guidelines","reason":"Full review of the SHA-matched 93-line pinned raw, accepted receipt/candidate, exact quote locations, fixed Q1/Q2 and Capital concept route found one material scope ambiguity. In the Capital-specific table, raw line 84's Pricing and eligibility row requires submission of eligibility criteria to allow users to request Capital; it does not say Capital pricing or fees are submitted for approval. The candidate and Q2 combine 'pricing and eligibility' with 'approval,' which could be read as an approval path for extra Capital fees contrary to the separately captured financial-products pricing guide's no-extra-Capital-fee rule."}
{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"docs-platforms-us-tax-forms","reason":"Independently read all 99 pinned raw lines, verified SHA-256 017ff472c14a0ff990a0ada158c24a87663db5982fb792bfafbd8f0eb4f95835 and all five exact quotes at their cited lines. Accepted receipt source and suggestions match candidate files; the Adyen Platforms route and concept ## Sources anchor exist. The entry correctly preserves the 1099-NEC title/body mismatch, US direct-payout scope, tax-year-2025 and state qualifications, TopUp exclusion, separate Customer Area/API credentials, and filing-versus-retrieval-versus-delivery boundary. One material internal conflict remains unmarked: raw line 24 says the 2025 federal threshold is USD 20,000 AND 200 transactions, while raw line 34 says a form is generated when a volume OR transaction threshold is met. The candidate presents both as operative statements without flagging their incompatible trigger logic. For Q1/Q2 this could yield a wrong 2025 filing/generation decision, and the raw route alone does not tell a reader which formulation controls."}
